      "empiricalEvidence": "Observed 2026-07-28 on this Mac with `codesign -d --entitlements :-`. Six shipping applications carry this key with exactly the <bundle-id>-spks / <bundle-id>-spki pattern: Mimestream (com.mimestream.Mimestream, sandboxed, exactly these two values and nothing else), WhatsApp (net.whatsapp.WhatsApp, sandboxed, exactly these two), Slipbox (ai.slipbox.macos.app, sandboxed, exactly these two), BoltAI and AdGuard Mini (sandboxed, these two plus unrelated product endpoints), and Tailscale (io.tailscale.ipn.macsys). Tailscale is the same Developer ID packet-tunnel host cited in the App Sandbox row: it carries the Sparkle exception while com.apple.security.app-sandbox is false, which is consistent — the exception is inert without the sandbox, and this project's host IS sandboxed. Sparkle 2's framework binary in Mimestream.app contains three endpoint suffixes, -spks, -spki and -spkp; only the first two are entitled here, matching the spec's decision to leave the Downloader, Installer Connection, and Installer Status services disabled. The two-value set is therefore deliberate, not a truncation.",
      "notAnEntitlement": "SUEnableInstallerLauncherService is an Info.plist key, not an entitlement, so it is out of this contract's scope. TASK-260715-uyju7n owns setting it on the generated macos.host target, and TASK-260717-xempiv owns the Sparkle integration that consumes it."
    }
  },
  "osVersionScope": null,
  "reopensOnly": null
}
{
  "key": "macos.provider",
  "bundleIdentifier": "works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel",
  "platform": "macOS",
  "role": "provider",
  "embeddedIn": "macos.host",
  "entitlements": {
    "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension": {
      "status": "required",
      "valueByChannel": {
        "development": [
          "packet-tunnel-provider"
        ],
        "developer-id": [
          "packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension"
        ]
      },
      "portalCapability": true,
      "portalCapabilityName": "Network Extensions",
      "rationale": "NEPacketTunnelProvider delivered as a system extension, which is what a directly distributed macOS packet-tunnel provider requires. The value follows rule NE1 and is identical to the host's on the same channel."
    },
    "com.apple.developer.system-extension.install": {
      "status": "prohibited",
      "portalCapability": false,
      "rationale": "The provider never installs an extension. Granting it would be privilege with no function. Confirmed against all three shipping Developer ID providers inspected on this Mac: none carries it."
    },
    "com.apple.security.application-groups": {
      "status": "prohibited",
      "portalCapability": false,
      "rationale": "Withheld under least privilege (rule G4): no grant this entitlement confers on macOS is used by the selected design. The six grants were read out of the system App Sandbox profile rather than assumed - three are home-relative and break across the root/user boundary, three are system-wide and belong to mechanisms this project does not use (Mac App Store group content, a group-prefixed XPC Mach service, group-prefixed POSIX IPC). The host-to-provider channel selected by TASK-260728-7ii1xz is providerConfiguration plus a sendProviderMessage seed into a provider-owned system-domain keychain item, and its XPC candidate - the only one that would have needed a group-prefixed Mach name - was rejected. A private group container is redundant with the container every sandboxed target already gets unconditionally (application.sb:107-108).",
      "evidence": "/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71): the entitlement appears at lines 302-330 and 459-465 and nowhere else. Full per-grant survey in appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule.survey.",
      "reopensOnly": "This row reopens if, and only if, one of three things becomes true, each of which is a grant in the survey above rather than a general appeal to App Groups: (a) the macOS host-to-provider channel becomes a group-prefixed Mach service, i.e. the XPC candidate is revived; (b) it becomes a group-prefixed POSIX IPC object; or (c) the provider is moved out of root into a user context, which would make the group container genuinely shared. It does NOT reopen because a spec sentence mentions App Groups, because a shipping third-party product carries the key, or to spare a portal sitting. Reopening is an amendment under section 11, naming which of (a), (b) or (c) became true.",
      "decidedBy": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo r4, on TASK-260728-7ii1xz"
    },
    "keychain-access-groups": {
      "status": "prohibited",
      "portalCapability": false,
      "rationale": "Settled by TASK-260728-7ii1xz. The selected transport is the file-based system-domain keychain, whose access-control model is per-item SecAccess ACLs, not access groups (Apple TN3137: 'The file-based keychain uses access control lists (SecAccess). The data protection keychain uses keychain access groups.'). The entitlement is therefore INAPPLICABLE to the mechanism actually used, not merely unnecessary. The provider needs no keychain entitlement at all: application.sb:792-793 already grants /Library/Keychains read+write to any process holding com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension, and application.sb:655-656 grant the securityd/SecurityServer Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. No new entitlement, no new App ID, no new portal capability, no change to Ceremony C1.",
      "evidence": "Observed 2026-07-28 on this Mac via codesign -d --entitlements :- on the installed system extensions: PureVPN's providers carry keychain-access-groups, Surfshark's carries a temporary-exception path for /Library/Keychains/. Both observations stand and neither is a requirement argument - see rule K1. Re-verified independently for r4 at /System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71): application.sb:792-793 (NE-entitled processes get /Library/Keychains read+write) and application.sb:655-656 (com.apple.securityd.xpc and com.apple.SecurityServer Mach lookups allowed unconditionally). Checks E8-E10 in TASK-260728-7ii1xz_macos-credential-transport-decision.md.",
      "decidedBy": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo r4, on TASK-260728-7ii1xz",
      "reopensOnly": "Only if the macOS provider is ever moved out of root into a user context, where the Data Protection Keychain exists and access groups mean something. Touching a keychain is not a reopening condition - see rule K1. Rule K3 carries the one further case this row does not settle: if the base App Sandbox profile on the oldest supported macOS does not grant /Library/Keychains to Network Extension processes, the provider needs a temporary FILE EXCEPTION - not this entitlement - and that case is armed through exceptionEntitlementRule.conditionalExceptions, never by re-granting this row.",
      "osVersionScope": {
        "rule": "K3",
        "verifiedOn": "macOS 26.5 (25F71)",
        "unverifiedAtFloor": "macOS 15.0",
        "claim": "The 'no keychain entitlement and no file exception' half of this row is verified only on macOS 26.5 (25F71). It is UNVERIFIED on the binding macOS 15.0 deployment floor. The 'keychain-access-groups is inapplicable' half is version-independent and is not in scope here - that half rests on the access-control model of the file-based keychain, not on a sandbox profile.",
        "resolutionOwner": "TASK-260715-1uxx3i",
        "ifAbsentAtFloor": "Arm exceptionEntitlementRule.conditionalExceptions entry X1-C1 under rule K3. Do NOT reject the exception as drift, and do NOT re-grant keychain-access-groups: an absent sandbox grant is an argument for a file exception, never for an access group the file-based keychain does not read."
      }
    },
    "com.apple.security.app-sandbox": {
      "status": "required-adjacent",
      "value": true,
      "portalCapability": false,
      "owner": "TASK-260715-uyju7n",
      "requirementSource": "apple-requirement",
      "requirementBasis": "Apple requires the Network Extension provider system extension to be sandboxed.",
      "rationale": "Unlike the host, the provider's sandbox is not a project choice. Verified 2026-07-28 on this Mac: all three shipping Developer ID packet-tunnel system extensions inspected carry com.apple.security.app-sandbox = true (Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark), while their containing apps carry false.",
      "portalCapabilityNote": "App Sandbox is a build-time entitlement, not a portal App ID capability; it is excluded from the Ceremony C1 scope."
    },
    "com.apple.security.network.client": {
      "status": "required-adjacent",
      "value": true,
      "portalCapability": false,
      "owner": "TASK-260715-uyju7n",
      "requirementSource": "sandbox-consequence",
      "requirementBasis": "Follows from the Apple-required provider sandbox above.",
      "rationale": "Outbound SSH to the user-controlled exit host."
    },
    "com.apple.security.network.server": {
      "status": "required-adjacent",
      "value": true,
      "portalCapability": false,
      "owner": "TASK-260715-uyju7n",
      "requirementSource": "sandbox-consequence",
      "requirementBasis": "Follows from the Apple-required provider sandbox above.",
      "rationale": "The internal HEV SOCKS boundary binds a loopback listener inside the provider; a sandboxed process cannot bind without it. It is not a user-reachable proxy."
    },
    "com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write": {
      "status": "conditional-pending-decision",
      "portalCapability": false,
      "requirementSource": "sandbox-consequence",
      "requirementBasis": "The provider is sandboxed because Apple requires a Network Extension provider system extension to be. Whether that sandbox already grants /Library/Keychains, or whether the grant must be requested as a temporary exception, is a property of the base profile on the running OS - which is why this row exists and why it is not yet authored.",
      "rationale": "UNARMED. On macOS 26.5 (25F71) this exception is unnecessary: application.sb lines 792-793 already grant /Library/Keychains read and write to any Network Extension-entitled process, so authoring it would be a least-privilege regression on the verified OS. On the binding macOS 15.0 floor the grant is unverified, so the row cannot be settled prohibited either - that is exactly the silent rejection reviewer verdict 08 F1 named. It is therefore modelled as conditional and pre-authorized: not in any allowlist, absent from every signed bundle, and armable on evidence alone. Governed by rule K3; register entry X1-C1.",
      "valuesIfArmed": [
        "/Library/Keychains/"
      ],
      "channelsIfArmed": [
        "development",
        "developer-id"
      ],
      "resolutionOwner": "TASK-260715-1uxx3i",
      "reopensOnly": "Not applicable while unarmed - this row IS the open question. It closes in one of two directions under rule K3: armed if the floor's base profile lacks the grant, retired if it carries it. Neither direction touches keychain-access-groups, any portal record, or the Ceremony C1 scope.",
      "evidence": "Absence of need on macOS 26.5 is read directly from /System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb lines 792-793 and 655-656. The floor has no reading; see rule K3 unverifiedAtFloor for why it could not be taken in this task's environment."
    }
  },
  "osVersionScope": null,
  "reopensOnly": null
}
{
  "key": "ios.host",
  "bundleIdentifier": "works.relux.tunnel.ios",
  "platform": "iOS",
  "role": "host",
  "embeddedIn": null,
  "entitlements": {
    "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension": {
      "status": "required",
      "valueByChannel": {
        "development": [
          "packet-tunnel-provider"
        ],
        "app-store": [
          "packet-tunnel-provider"
        ]
      },
      "portalCapability": true,
      "portalCapabilityName": "Network Extensions",
      "rationale": "The host manages NETunnelProviderManager configurations on iOS. Neither iOS channel is direct Developer ID signing, so rule NE1 gives the unsuffixed value on both."
    },
    "com.apple.developer.system-extension.install": {
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "portalCapability": false,
      "rationale": "System extensions do not exist on iOS."
    },
    "com.apple.security.application-groups": {
      "status": "required",
      "value": [
        "group.works.relux.tunnel"
      ],
      "portalCapability": true,
      "portalCapabilityName": "App Groups",
      "rationale": "Granted: on iOS the host and the appex run as the same user in the same context, so the registered iOS-style record (rule G1) is a genuinely shared filesystem container and UserDefaults suite. That is the concrete, current function rule G4 requires - the host publishes the non-secret profile snapshot and the appex reads it (.spec/architecture.md:111; consumer TASK-260715-3f4lxy). Provisioning is deferred with the rest of iOS under ADR-024; the contract is not.",
      "functionOnThisPlatform": "shared group container and UserDefaults suite"
    },
    "keychain-access-groups": {
      "status": "required",
      "value": [
        "$(AppIdentifierPrefix)works.relux.tunnel.shared"
      ],
      "portalCapability": false,
      "rationale": "Data Protection Keychain vault owner.",
      "defaultAccessGroup": "262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared"
    },
    "com.apple.security.app-sandbox": {
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "portalCapability": false,
      "requirementSource": "platform-inherent",
      "requirementBasis": "iOS apps are always sandboxed; the entitlement does not exist as an opt-in.",
      "rationale": "Not an authored key on iOS."
    }
  },
  "osVersionScope": null,
  "reopensOnly": null
}
